  "hints": [
    "Only the most frequent label really forces idle time. Picture its copies laid out with gaps between them.",
    "With maxCount copies of the busiest label you get maxCount - 1 full frames of length gap + 1, plus a final frame holding every label that ties for maxCount.",
    "Answer = max(len(jobs), (maxCount - 1) · (gap + 1) + tiedCount). The max handles schedules with so many labels that no idle slot is needed."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function scheduleLength(jobs, gap) {\n  const counts = new Map()\n  for (const job of jobs) counts.set(job, (counts.get(job) ?? 0) + 1)\n  let maxCount = 0\n  let tied = 0\n  for (const count of counts.values()) {\n    if (count > maxCount) {\n      maxCount = count\n      tied = 1\n    } else if (count === maxCount) tied++\n  }\n  return Math.max(jobs.length, (maxCount - 1) * (gap + 1) + tied)\n}\n",
    "py": "from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef schedule_length(jobs, gap):\n    if not jobs:\n        return 0\n    counts = Counter(jobs).values()\n    max_count = max(counts)\n    tied = sum(1 for count in counts if count == max_count)\n    return max(len(jobs), (max_count - 1) * (gap + 1) + tied)\n",
    "explanation": "The busiest label needs its copies at least gap + 1 apart, so it alone forces (maxCount - 1) · (gap + 1) + 1 units, and every other label tied at maxCount adds one more unit to the last frame. Other jobs fill the idle slots inside the frames; if they overflow, frames can be widened so no idle time remains, and the answer is just the number of jobs. The larger of the two bounds is always achievable."
  },
